WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
Loading an assembly loads code into the process.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:08.360
That makes plugin and extension systems a trust decision, not just a file handling decision.

3
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:16.700
A plugin can run with access to the process, configuration, services, and host capabilities that the application makes available.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:24.500
AssemblyLoadContext can isolate dependencies and support unloading, but it is not a complete security boundary for untrusted code.

5
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:33.320
It can help manage version conflicts and plugin lifetime, yet the loaded code is still running in the same process model and needs a trusted governance path.

6
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:43.840
Do not load arbitrary assemblies from user-controlled paths, uploads, temporary folders, package feeds, or configuration without a trusted review process.

7
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:52.020
A plugin system should define how code is obtained, approved, versioned, configured, deployed, monitored, and retired.

8
00:00:52.300 --> 00:01:02.620
Plugin systems may use signed packages, controlled directories, allowlisted plugin IDs, version review, dependency review, and operational ownership where appropriate.

9
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.600
A plugin contract should expose only the capabilities the plugin needs.

10
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:17.940
Broad access to service providers, secrets, file systems, network clients, database connections, or administrative APIs should not be the default.

11
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:26.620
Dependency conflicts, abandoned plugins, stale packages, and unsafe update channels should be reviewed as supply-chain risks.

12
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:36.080
A plugin architecture remains a production code path, so it needs ownership, change control, logging, rollback expectations, and a clear decommissioning plan.
